## a-net row

Finding R11 is gone. The hoop rim is an open ring of two thin parallel lines. The white strip
between the two lines is one closed channel (bbox 170,25 to 424,299 at 1920 x 1280). The strip
measures 7 px at the narrowest row, 9 px at the median and 15 px at the 90th percentile. The
outline lines measure 7 to 17 px. On the 4000 px print the strip measures 14 to 36 px.

Strip judgment: no line gap. Flood fill from the frame never reaches the strip. The strip width
stays near the line width on every row. It never closes to black. A child can color the strip or
leave it white, and no color can run out of it. Line crossings at shallow angles leave 1 to 4 px
white wedges beside the strip; each wedge stays closed.

Goal check: Nan holds up a green butterfly net in a garden. Both files show this. The raised hand
grips the handle. The hoop, the bag and the net grid match the color file. Both eye rings are
closed with solid pupils. Lash flicks show at both outer corners. The gripping hand shows three
finger lobes, one thumb bump and one finger behind the handle. The hanging hand matches the
color mitten. Face, shirt, shorts, shoes, hands, handle tube, hoop cells and bag cells are all
closed regions.

Facts recorded, not defects: the line file draws no nose line, the color file has a soft nose
bump; the face reads whole. A solid area scan of the line file finds only the two pupils and one
eyebrow band as solid black; no ring and no blob exists. One scan row at the hoop bottom returns
a 51 px black run because the inner rim line runs near-horizontal there. The zoom shows two
normal lines with the white channel between them. The line file draws three finger lobes where
the color file draws one smooth finger mass; these are short inner detail lines.

